**Comment-2:** Theorem 3 needs too many assumptions so that I think it cannot be used to many loss functions.
* **Response:** Thanks for your comment. In fact, the assumptions in Theorem 3 can be satisfied withmany common used loss functions (such as Mean Square Error, Huber Loss, Cross-entropy loss, etc).  I will take Cross-entropy loss as an example for illustration and discussion. Let $h_{\theta}(x)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-\theta^{T}x}}$, then the Cross-entropy loss function and its first-order derivative, second-order derivative and third-order derivative are written as follows:
\begin{equation}
L(\theta) = -\left(y^T \log h_{\theta}(x)+(1-y^T)\log(1-h_{\theta}(x))\right)
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial L(\theta)}{\partial \theta} = -\left(h_{\theta}(x)-y^T\right)x
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial^2 L(\theta)}{\partial \theta^2} = -\frac{\partial h_{\theta}(x)}{\partial \theta}x
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial^3 L(\theta)}{\partial \theta^3} = -\frac{\partial^2 h_{\theta}(x)}{\partial \theta^2}x
\end{equation}
In general, $\theta$ is bounded due to the regularizer added in objective function. The true label $y$ is also bounded and the feature $x$ is usually rescaled within [0,1] or [-1,1]. Thus, we can easily get that $\frac{\partial L(\theta)}{\partial \theta}$ and $h_{\theta}(x)$ are all bounded by some constant. Then we bound the second-order derivative and third-order derivative. It is easy to get that $\frac{\partial h_{\theta}(x)}{\partial \theta}=\frac{(-x)e^{-\theta x}}{(1+e^{-\theta x})^2}$. Since $\theta$ and $x$ are bounded, $\frac{\partial h_{\theta}(x)}{\partial \theta}$ would be consequently bounded. Similarly, we can prove that \frac{\partial^2 h_{\theta}(x)}{\partial \theta^2} is bouned too. To sum up, we have that the Cross-entropy loss function can satisfy our assumptions in Theorem 3 under general considerations.
The quadratic loss functions, such as Mean Square Error, are more likely to satisfy the assumptions, since its second-order derivative is a constant and third-order derivative is zero.

Given the probability density function $f(b)=\frac{1}{\alpha}e^{-\beta_{\epsilon}  ||b||}$, which is in (3), and in Chaudhuri et al. Since the function is symmetric with respect to the y-axis, we can get its CDF by integrating it on both sides of the y-axis as follows:

\begin{equation}
F(b) = 
\begin{cases}
(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}}) + \frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}} e^{\beta_{\epsilon} b} ,  & \mbox{if } b \le 0, \\\\
(\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}}) - \frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}} e^{-\beta_{\epsilon} b} ,  & \mbox{if } b > 0.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

Note that for any probability density function $f(x)$, we have $\int_{- \infty}^{+ \infty}f(x)dx=1$. Thus, we have $\int_{0}^{+ \infty}(\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}}) - \frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}} e^{-\beta_{\epsilon} b}db=\frac{1}{2}$. Since $\lim_{b \to 0}e^{-\beta_{\epsilon} b}=1$ and $\lim_{b \to + \infty}e^{-\beta_{\epsilon} b}=0$, we have that $\frac{1}{\alpha \beta_{\epsilon}}=\frac{1}{2}$. Substitute it into above Equation, we will get Equation (11). Thus, (11) is exactly the CDF of (3), which is the same as in Chaudhuri et al.

If there is any problem in the above derivation, please point out.
